---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { LINKS, MCP_PATH } from '../data/six10nine';

const config = JSON.stringify({ mcpServers: { six10nine: { command: MCP_PATH } } }, null, 2);
const brewConfig = JSON.stringify({ mcpServers: { six10nine: { command: 'six10nine-mcp' } } }, null, 2);
const example = JSON.stringify(
  { app: 'com.apple.Safari', chord: '⌘8', menu_path: ['Window', 'Show Tab Overview'] },
  null,
  2,
);

const tools = [
  ['list_apps', 'Read', 'Apps that have chords, by bundle ID, with how many.'],
  ['list_chords', 'Read', 'One app’s chords, or every app’s, and what each does.'],
  ['get_chord', 'Read', 'One chord in one app, and what it does in other apps.'],
  ['set_chord_action', 'Write', 'Make a chord click a menu item (["View", "Command Palette…"]). Replaces the chord’s action in that app.'],
  ['remove_chord', 'Write', 'Remove a chord’s action in an app.'],
  ['get_aura_status', 'Read', 'Status from the app: aura, whether chords are on, and today’s idle computing.'],
];
---

<Base title="MCP server" description="six10nine-mcp lets Cursor, Claude and other AI agents read and change your six10nine chords." docs>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1>MCP server</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The app bundles <code>six10nine-mcp</code>, a small <a href={LINKS.mcp}>Model Context Protocol</a> server, so AI
    agents can read and change your chords.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Speaks stdio, MCP protocol version 2025-06-18 (it also answers clients on 2025-03-26 and 2024-11-05).</li>
    <li>Reads and writes only six10nine’s own settings on your Mac. It never opens a network connection.</li>
    <li>Changes take effect right away: the running app reloads chords when the server changes them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Set up</h2>
  <p>
    Add it to Cursor (<code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code>), Claude Desktop (<code>claude_desktop_config.json</code>) or any
    MCP client:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{config}</code></pre>
  <p>
    If you installed to <code>~/Applications</code>, use that path. With Homebrew the command is also linked as
    <code>six10nine-mcp</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{brewConfig}</code></pre>

  <h2>Tools</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th></th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {tools.map(([name, kind, does]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{name}</code></td>
            <td class="kind">{kind}</td>
            <td>{does}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Apps are named by bundle ID, like <code>com.apple.Safari</code>; <code>list_apps</code> shows the ones with chords.
    For <code>set_chord_action</code>, give <code>menu_path</code>, the menu item’s titles from the menu bar down. Titles
    must match the app’s menus exactly. Chords only run menu items, so a keyboard shortcut can’t be set.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>

  <h2>Chord notation</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Written as</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Together</td><td><code>⌘8</code>, <code>⌘⇧8K</code>, <code>cmd+shift+8+k</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>In order</td><td><code>⌘P·O·O·P</code>, <code>cmd+p&gt;o&gt;o&gt;p</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Keys are letters, digits, and <code>`</code> for the key left of 1.</p>

  <h2>Trust</h2>
  <p>
    The server only talks to the process that started it, over stdio. Treat an MCP client that runs it as trusted to
    change your chords.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .kind {
    color: var(--text-faint);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
</style>
